Abstract
Zearalenone (ZEN) is a widespread nonsteroidal mycotoxin with estrogen-like activity. Sensitive and reliable quantification of ZEN in food is critical to ensure food safety and safeguard agricultural production. Herein, by combining the metal-organic frameworks-based nanozyme (MOFzyme) and hyaluronic acid (HA)-DNA hydrogel, a well-designed colorimetric aptasensor was developed. The HA-DNA hydrogel was deposited on the surface of the bimetallic MOFzyme via strand-induced hybridization chain reaction (HCR). Relying on the ZEN-specific aptamer integrated in hydrogel structure, the disintegration of the hydrogel network and the concomitant exposure of the encapsulated MOFzyme could be specifically triggered by the introduction of ZEN molecules. Moreover, the magnitude of hydrogel disintegration was positively correlated with the amount of ZEN, by which the quantification of ZEN can be effortlessly achieved. By combining the delicate design, the satisfactory catalytic performance and stability of bimetallic MOFzyme and the appealing stimuli-responsiveness of DNA hydrogel, the developed aptasensor demonstrated superior analytical performance and ease of use. Under optimal conditions, the linear range of the aptasensor fell between 0.001 and \(200~\mathrm{ng~mL^{-1}}\) with a limit of detection (LOD) of \(0.8~\mathrm{pg~mL^{-1}}\). Furthermore, the aptasensor was successfully applied for the quantitative detection of ZEN in corn and soybean samples with recoveries ranging from \(94.0\%\) to \(109.0\%\). The developed aptasensor
is expected to be a prospective universal platform for accurately quantifying food or environmental hazards.
Keywords: Zeavalenone; Metal-organic frameworks; Nanozyme; DNA hydrogel
## 1. Introduction
High-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC), alone or in combination with mass spectrometry (HPLC-MS), is considered the universal identification method for ZEN (Caglayan et al., 2020). They certainly possess satisfactory selectivity and
sensitivity. However, the demand for sophisticated instruments and expensive consumables greatly limited their widespread use in economically underdeveloped areas. In addition, the relatively poor availability and cost-effectiveness of antibodies also prevented the broader adoption of enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA). Hence, developing a simple, rapid and cost-effective method for the quantitative determination of ZEN in food is undoubtedly a top priority.
Recently, stimuli-responsive hydrogels have gained growing interest in the domain of biosensing due to their excellent biocompatibility, satisfactory stimuli-responsiveness, and controllable loading capacity (Fischer et al., 2020; Won et al., 2020; Yang et al., 2022). DNA hydrogels, usually as networks of polymer chains, are cross-linked by physical entanglement or chemical bonding between DNA chains. Numerous studies have shown that DNA-based hydrogels not only retain the biological functions of DNA, but also inherit the skeletal functions of hydrogels (Yang et al., 2021; Zhao et al., 2021). In typical applications, DNA hydrogel-based biosensors employ polymers as scaffolds, and various functional DNA motifs as the cross-linkers and recognition probes (Chen et al., 2018; Vazquez-Gonzalez and Willner, 2020). The cross-linkers dissociate exclusively upon recognizing the corresponding stimulus, triggering the disintegration of the hydrogel and the concomitant exposure of the pre-encapsulated cargo (English et al., 2019). Combining this property with different readout techniques, such as colorimetry,
microfluidic chips, glucose meters, manometers, pH meters, etc., allows the development of sensitive biosensors for different targets.
Natural enzymes can efficiently and selectively catalyze biochemical reactions and play an essential role in many biological processes. However, the applicability of natural enzymes is often limited by their inherent drawbacks, such as high preparation and purification costs, low stability, and difficulty in functional modification and recovery (Wang et al., 2021). To circumvent these limitations, nanozymes with intrinsic enzyme-like activities similar to natural enzymes have emerged as low-cost and highly stable alternatives to natural enzymes (Jiang et al., 2019; Xu et al., 2019; Zhang et al., 2018). Nevertheless, most of the currently explored nanozymes can only mimic biological enzymes in catalytic function, while differing significantly from the latter in terms of structure and even composition. The lack of fine structure around the active center results in the scarcity of catalytic specificity of these nanozymes to provide the necessary selectivity during target detection (Yu et al., 2021). In this context, the integration of nanozymes with aptamer who combines the functions of target recognition and enzyme activity regulation would essentially solve the thorny problem of insufficient selectivity. To date, a considerable number of biosensors have emerged based on the enzyme activity regulation effect of aptamer on nanozymes (Chen et al., 2020; Wu et al., 2021). They have made a splash in the fields of clinical diagnostics (Huang et al., 2018),
environmental monitoring (Zhang et al., 2022; Zhu et al., 2019) and food safety (Han et al., 2022), which may be due to their outstanding stability and ease of fabrication. However, this ssDNA-nanozymes-based sensing system also has its shortcomings. For example, due to the existence of steric hindrance and the complexity of the interaction forces between the nanozyme and aptamer, aptamer may not be effectively adsorbed; In addition, certain structural changes in aptamer resulting from target binding may not be sufficient to remove aptamer from the surface of nanozymes. The presence of all these factors may affect the sensitivity of the biosensors.
Herein, inspired by the above surveys, a novel colorimetric aptasensor for sensitively and precisely quantifying ZEN was developed based on a metal-organic frameworks-based nanozyme (MOFzyme) and hyaluronic acid (HA)-DNA hydrogel. A bimetallic MOFzyme with excellent oxidase activity was primarily obtained by partially oxidizing the Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF. Hereafter, the stimuli-responsive HA-DNA hydrogel was deposited on the surface of the bimetallic MOFzyme by the strand-induced hybridization chain reaction (HCR). The constructed DNA hydrogel ideally preserved the biological function of DNA, and was thus endowed with exceptional stimuli-responsiveness. Relying on a validated ZEN-specific aptamer (Bi et al., 2021; Khan et al., 2019), the disintegration of the hydrogel network structure and the concomitant exposure of the encapsulated MOFzyme could be
specifically triggered by the presence of ZEN to a magnitude that positively correlated with the level of ZEN content. Therefore, the quantitative detection of ZEN can be achieved by utilizing the stimuli-responsiveness of DNA hydrogel and the regulation effect of hydrogel coating on MOFzyme oxidase activity. In short, an aptasensor developed based on a DNA hydrogel-coated bimetallic MOFzyme debuted in biosensing. Combining the exquisite design, the good catalytic ability and stability of bimetallic MOFzyme as well as the stimuli-responsiveness of HA-DNA hydrogel, the well-designed colorimetric aptasensor was endowed with excellent analytical performance and ease of use.
## 2. Materials and Methods
## 2.1 Chemicals and materials
The ZEN aptamer used in this study was screened through the capture-SELEX strategy with a dissociation constant \((K_{\mathrm{d}})\) of \(41 \pm 5\) nM and a sufficient specificity (Chen et al., 2013). The sequence is as follows: 5'-TCATCTATCTATGGTACATTACTATCTGTAATGTGATATG-3'. All the oligonucleotides were synthesized and purified by Sangon Bioengineering Technology and Services Co. Ltd (Shanghai, China), and the detailed information was summarized in Table S1.
Additional materials, reagents, apparatus and detailed procedures for synthesizing
pristine and oxidized Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF, for synthesizing azide-functionalized and nucleic acid (1)/(2)-functionalized bimetallic MOFzyme, sample preparation as well as quantitative analysis were given in the Supporting Information (Section 1).
## 2.2 Synthesis of hyaluronic acid-modified oligo/copolymers
Amino-modified nucleic acid (3) and hairpin (5) were attached to the HA backbone to form copolymers HA-(3) and HA-(5), respectively. In order to activate HA, EDC (0.19 mmol) and NHS (0.05 mmol) were added to the \(1\%\) HA solution. Following that, \(100~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) nucleic acid (3) or hairpin (5) was reacted with \(400~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) activated HA, and the final concentration of (3) or (5) was \(100~\upmu\mathrm{M}\). After \(12~\mathrm{h}\) of incubation, copolymers HA-(3) and HA-(5) were obtained. The HA-(3) and HA-(5) were washed thrice with ultrapure water and purified once by ultrafiltration to discard the unreacted substance. The UV spectroscopy was adopted to quantify the amount of oligonucleotide (3) in polymer HA-(3), and oligonucleotide (4) of equimolar amount was then added. The mixture of HA-(3) and oligonucleotide (4) was incubated at \(37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(4~\mathrm{h}\) to realize effective cross-linking and generate copolymer HA-(3)/(4). Notably, the HA-modified copolymer chain HA-(3)/(4) and HA-(5) were each functionalized with a DNA hairpin (hairpin (4) or (5)), and each hairpin contained a ZEN-specific aptamer.
## 2.3 Synthesis of hyaluronic acid-DNA hydrogel-coated MOFzyme
The promoter unit located inside the nucleic acid (2) can trigger the HCR process,
thereby forming a HA hydrogel coating on the surface of the MOFzyme. In detail, HA-(3)/(4) (0.5 mL), HA-(5) (0.5 mL) were mixed with (1)/(2)-functionalized MOFzyme (1 mL), wherein the nucleic acid (2), hairpin (4) and hairpin (5) were of 5, 10, and 10 \(\upmu\)M, respectively. The mixture was incubated overnight at \(40^{\circ}\)C and 800 rpm. Afterwards, it was washed with PBS thrice to discard the polymers not involved in the cross-linking. The resulting HA-DNA hydrogel-coated MOFzyme was kept in PBS at \(4^{\circ}\)C for future use.
## 3. Results and Discussion
3.1 Scheme for the quantification of ZEN
173
Scheme 1. Schematic illustration of the construction and application of the aptasensor developed based on the bimetallic MOFzyme and hyaluronic acid-DNA
hydrogel.
The pristine Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF was obtained via solvothermal reaction, followed by being partially oxidized to a mixed-valence bimetallic MOFzyme. The scheme for assembling and applying the developed HA-DNA hydrogel-coated MOFzyme was depicted in Scheme 1. In detail, nucleic acid (1) was conjugated to MOFzyme via a triazine bridge. Hereafter, with the help of the promoter unit contained in the nucleic acid (2), a strand-induced HCR occurred within the duplex capping units (hybrid duplex (1)/(2)), HA-modified copolymer HA-(3)/(4) and copolymer HA-(5), thereby coating the surface of MOFzyme with a HA-DNA hydrogel. The promoter unit in nucleic acid (2) can interact with the hairpin (4), thus unfolding (4) and exposing the rest of the hairpin (4). Following this, an inter-chain bridging unit will be produced between the exposed region of the two hairpins. The unfolding of the hairpin (5) will proceed to unfold the remaining hairpin (4). Accordingly, as the HCR reaction progressed, a hydrogel coating was gradually deposited on the surface of the MOFzyme. Interestingly, a ZEN-specific aptamer sequence was comprised in the DNA hydrogel structure. In the presence of ZEN, the formation of the ZEN/aptamer complex will induce the dissociation of the copolymer chains, which in turn will lead to the disintegration of the HA-DNA hydrogel and the exposure of MOFzyme. The magnitude of hydrogel disintegration was positively correlated with the target amount in the system. Furthermore, the \(\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}\) with a
standard electrode potential of \(-2.32\,\mathrm{~V~}\) in the bimetallic MOFzyme can reduce oxygen dissolved in the buffer to superoxide radicals, while \(\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}\) itself would be oxidized to \(\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}\). Both superoxide radicals and \(\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}\) possessed the ability to catalyze the oxidation of TMB to produce the blue chromogenic product. Thus, the amount of ZEN in the system can be quantified by the absorbance of the chromogenic product.
3.2 Characterization of the pristine and oxidized Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF
Fig. 1. Characterization of the pristine and oxidized Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF. SEM images of the pristine (A) and oxidized Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF (B). PXRD patterns (C) and FT-IR spectra (D) of the as-synthesized pristine (black) and oxidized Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF (red).
The pristine Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF was synthesized via the hydrothermal method with \( \mathrm{Zr}^{4+} \) and \( \mathrm{Ce}^{4+} \) as metal precursors, while \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \mathrm{BDC} \) served as the coordination ligand. Subsequently, the pristine Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF was partially oxidized using the mixed solution of NaOH and \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2} \). The morphologies of pristine and oxidized Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF were characterized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM), and both of them exhibited a typical octahedral morphology (Fig. 1A and B). Furthermore, the powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of the two materials (Fig. 1C) were consistent with the simulation curve of UiO-66-\( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \) (Zr) deposited in the Cambridge Crystallographic Data Centre (No. 1405751). A series of fine diffraction peaks were still observed in the pattern of oxidized bimetallic MOF, so the oxidation treatment did not significantly alter the surface morphology and crystal structure of Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF. In the Fourier-transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra of the two materials (Fig. 1D), the tensile vibration peaks of \( \mathrm{C}=\mathrm{C} \) and \( \mathrm{C}-\mathrm{C} \) on the benzene ring appeared at 1498 and \( 1432\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1} \), respectively. The tensile vibration peak of \( \mathrm{Zr}/\mathrm{Ce}-\mathrm{O} \), which was originated from the secondary building units (SBUs) of MOFs, was observed at \( 669\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) in pristine Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF. However, it cannot be identified in the oxidized one, indicating the destruction of the internal structure of bimetallic MOF. Most importantly, in the two patterns, the amino group of \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \mathrm{BDC} \) brought about the peaks at 3470 and \( 3350\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) which could be assigned to \( \nu(\mathrm{NH}_{2}) \). The result demonstrated that the \( -\mathrm{NH}_{2} \) in bimetallic MOF was retained during the
partial oxidation treatment; Thus, the constructed MOFzyme can be functionalized by oligonucleotides.
3.3 Oxidase-like catalytic properties of the oxidized Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF
bimetallic MOF. The kinetics curve of oxidized Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF obtained by varying TMB from 0 to \(0.5~\mathrm{mM}\) at \(\mathrm{pH}\,7.0\) and \(25\mathrm{{^\circC}}\) (A), and the corresponding Lineweaver-Burk plot (B). Ce 3d XPS patterns of pristine (C) and oxidized Ce-Zr
bimetallic MOF before and after being exposed to TMB (D and E). EPR spectra measured for pristine (black) and oxidized Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF (red) using DMPO as a trap (F).
The oxidase-like activity of the oxidized Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF was evaluated using TMB as organic substrate. Considering the usage scenarios of the aptasensor, the evaluation was conducted in PBS (pH 7.0) at room temperature. As depicted in Fig. S1, both the pristine and oxidized Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF as well as UiO-66-NH\(_2\) (Ce) possessed the catalytic ability. The oxidized Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF obviously exhibited the most robust catalytic performance towards TMB. Moreover, the catalytic ability of pristine Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF was superior to that of UiO-66-NH\(_2\) (Ce). This may be attributed to the fact that the introduced bimetallic nodes created defects in the MOF structure, which in turn increased the number of its active sites and thus improved its catalytic performance (Jin et al., 2021). Moreover, UiO-66-NH\(_2\) (Zr) hardly demonstrated oxidase activity towards TMB, implying that the oxidase activity of Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF originated from the Ce\(^{4+}\)/Ce\(^{3+}\) nodes instead of the Zr\(^{4+}\) nodes.
The steady-state kinetic assays were performed in PBS at pH 7.0 and 25°C to quantify the catalytic ability of oxidized Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF. Michaelis-Menten curve (Fig. 2A) was obtained by varying the concentration of TMB in the range of 0~0.5 mM and measuring the reaction rate. From the double reciprocal plot (Fig. 2B),
the constant of Michaelis-Menten \(\left(K_{m}\right)\) and the maximal reaction velocity \(\left(V_{max}\right)\) were determined to be \(0.17\ \mathrm{mM}\) and \(17.6 \times 10^{-8}\ \mathrm{M\ s^{-1}}\), respectively. The oxidized Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF exhibited a smaller \(K_{m}\) for TMB than some reported nanozymes using TMB as a substrate (details in Table S2), which suggested that the oxidized Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF possessed a stronger affinity towards TMB. In conclusion, the synthesized oxidized Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF possessed desirable oxidase activity, and thus it can be used as a bimetallic MOFzyme and further functionalized for biosensing applications.
The batch-to-batch performance variation and long-term stability of the bimetallic MOFzyme, i.e., the oxidized Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF, were explored to validate its reliability. Five groups of bimetallic MOFzyme prepared independently via the same method were incubated with TMB (1 mM) for 20 min, and the relative standard deviation (RSD) between the absorbance they induced was \(1.54\%\). Therefore, the bimetallic MOFzyme possessed satisfactory reproducibility. The long-term stability was then assessed. Bimetallic MOFzyme from the same batch was kept at \(4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), and an equal amount of it was incubated with TMB (1 mM) for 20 min every five days. The bimetallic MOFzyme retained \(93.8\%\) of its initial catalytic activity after persistent storage for 30 days (Fig. S2). The reasonable stability of the bimetallic MOFzyme synthesized with \(\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}\) and \(\mathrm{Zr}^{4+}\) as metal precursors may arise from the high charge density and bond polarization of M(IV), conferring to M(IV) a strong
affinity for the O atoms in the carboxylic acids.
## 3.4 Catalysis mechanism of the bimetallic MOFzyme
To figure out the underlying catalytic mechanism of the bimetallic MOFzyme, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was performed on pristine Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF, bimetallic MOFzyme before being exposed to TMB, and bimetallic MOFzyme after being exposed to TMB for valence state analysis. As shown in the high-resolution Ce 3d spectra (Fig. 2C, D and E), the patterns could be well deconvoluted into multiple peaks corresponding to \(\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}\) and \(\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}\). The ratio of \(\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}\) to \(\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}\) was 0.21:1 in pristine Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF. After the oxidation treatment, the ratio was enhanced to 1.55:1, indicating that approximately \(52\%\) of the \(\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}\) was oxidized to \(\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}\). As for the oxidized one who was exposed to TMB for 120 min, that ratio was 1.48:1, which did not change significantly from the result obtained before the catalytic reaction. Hence, the bimetallic MOFzyme played the role of catalyst in the catalytic oxidation. Besides, intermediates that may be formed during catalytic reactions were identified by electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR). Apparent EPR signals which could be ascribed to superoxide radicals \(\left(\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\right)\)were observed in bimetallic MOFzyme when 5,5-dimethyl-1-pyrroline N-oxide (DMPO) was used as a trap, while no similar signal was observed in the pristine Ce-Zr bimetallic MOF (Fig. 2F). Based on the above-mentioned experimental results and reported research (Cheng et al., 2016), the potential process for the oxidation of TMB by oxidized Ce-
Zr bimetallic MOF can be speculated as follows:
\[
\mathrm{O}_2+\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}\longrightarrow\mathrm{O}_2^-+\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}
\]
\[
\cdot\mathrm{O}_2^-+\mathrm{TMB}\rightarrow\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}+\mathrm{oxTMB}
\]
\[
\mathrm{Ce^{4+}+TMB\rightarrow Ce^{3+}+oxTMB}
\]
Specifically, \(\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}\) in the MOF structure was oxidized to \(\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}\) while activating the oxygen dissolved in the buffer solution to generate superoxide radicals with strong oxidizing ability. Afterwards, superoxide radicals and \(\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}\) can simultaneously catalyze the oxidation of colorless TMB to blue oxTMB. In this step, \(\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}\) was regenerated to \(\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}\) and then spontaneously joined the cycle of this catalytic process. 3.5 Characterization of azide-functionalized, nucleic acid (1)/(2)-functionalized and hydrogel-coated MOFzyme
The azide-functionalized MOFzyme was subjected to XPS before and after it was functionalized by nucleic acid (1)/(2) to explore their elemental compositions and chemical states. The signals of Ce 3d, N 1s, C 1s and Zr 3d were observed in both samples (Fig. 3A). Besides, one additional signal ascribed to \(\mathrm{~P~}2\mathrm{p}\) appeared in the DNA-functionalized MOFzyme, which evidently originated from the conjugated nucleic acid (1)/(2). The \(\mathrm{~P~}2\mathrm{p}\) core-level spectrum could be well deconvoluted into two peaks at 133.92 and 134.82 eV, corresponding to 2p3/2 and 2p1/2, respectively (Fig. 3B). Simultaneously, the high-resolution C 1s spectra of azide-functionalized MOFzyme before and after DNA functionalization could be decomposed into three
peaks assignable to C−C/C=C, C−O/C−N and \(\mathrm{COO}^{-}\), respectively (Fig. 3C). Among them, the content of the C element in the form of C−O/C−N increased from \(4.75\%\) before DNA functionalization to \(9.74\%\) after functionalization, implying that the MOFzyme was successfully functionalized by nucleic acids.
