after interacting with ZEN at a concentration of \(100~\mathrm{ng~mL^{-1}}\) (A). Luminescence spectra of the redox reaction product under different combinations: bare bimetallic MOFzyme (a), DNA-functionalized MOFzyme (b), hydrogel-coated MOFzyme (c), ZEN of \(10~\mathrm{ng~mL^{-1}}\) (d) and hydrogel-coated MOFzyme with ZEN at a concentration of \(10~\mathrm{ng~mL^{-1}}\) (e). Except for group d, the content of MOFzyme in other groups was \(4~\upmu\mathrm{g}\) (B). Plot for optimizing the concentration of copolymers (C) and the crosslinking time between (1)/(2)-functionalized MOFzyme and copolymers (D).
Circular dichroism (CD) spectra and luminescence spectra were acquired to verify the feasibility of the developed aptasensor. As shown in Fig. 4A, after interacting with ZEN at a concentration of \(100~\mathrm{ng~mL^{-1}}\), the positive peak at \(272~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}\) and the negative peak at \(244~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}\) in the CD spectrum of hydrogel were blue-shifted to 279 and \(248~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}\), respectively. In addition, the intensity of the negative peak was somewhat weakened. These results implied that the introduction of the target can substantially alter the conformation of the nucleic acids that constitute the DNA hydrogel, leading to the disintegration of the hydrogel. The feasibility of the sensing platform was further corroborated by the luminescence spectra (Fig. 4B). After being incubated with TMB for \(30~\mathrm{min}\), the bare bimetallic MOFzyme \((4~\upmu\mathrm{g})\) demonstrated an absorbance of 0.59 at \(652~\mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 4B, curve a). The (1)/(2)-functionalized and hydrogel-coated MOFzyme containing the same mass of bimetallic MOFzyme exhibited the absorbance of 0.58 (Fig. 4B, curve b) and 0.16 (Fig. 4B, curve c). The
results implied that the conjugation of hybrid duplex (1)/(2) can affect the catalytic capacity of MOFzyme to a certain extent, while the hydrogel coating will further enhance the barrier between the MOFzyme and the organic substrate, leading to a more pronounced weakening of the absorbance. Hence, both hybrid duplex and hydrogel can modulate the oxidase activity of MOFzyme. Moreover, during the incubation of hydrogel-coated bimetallic MOFzyme with TMB, ZEN (10 ng mL\(^{-1}\)), which was not catalytic (Fig. 4B, curve d), was added and induced an intense response (Fig. 4B, curve e). These results again highlighted that the addition of the target can cause the hydrogel to disintegrate. All in all, it is feasible to achieve quantitative detection of ZEN using the stimuli-responsiveness of HA-DNA hydrogel.
## 3.7 Optimization of the experimental parameters
In the colorimetric sensing platform, the hydrogel generation conditions are crucial to obtain reliable detection results. Hence, the concentration of copolymers (defined as the concentrations of DNA hairpins functionalized on HA copolymers) and the cross-linking time were optimized successively (Fig. 4C and D). The optimal copolymer concentration and cross-linking time were determined to be 10 \(\upmu\)M and 10 h, respectively, and were used in the following experiment. The detailed optimization procedures were presented in Supporting Information (Section 2).3.8 Figures of merit for zearalenone quantification
The analytical performance of the developed sensing platform based on the hydrogel-coated bimetallic MOFzyme was assessed under optimal conditions. The absorbance of the system exhibited an obvious ascending trend with the increment of ZEN concentration (Fig. 5A). The changed absorbance of the system brought by the disintegration of hydrogel was highly dependent on the concentration of ZEN, setting the stage for the measurement of ZEN. A good linear calibration curve was obtained within the range of 0.001-200 ng mL\(^{-1}\) by plotting the changed absorbance against the ZEN concentration on logarithmic scales (Fig. 5B). The linear regression equation could be expressed as: \(\Delta\mathrm{Abs}=0.087\,\mathrm{lg}\,\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{ZEN}}+0.260\,(R^{2}=0.992)\). The limit of detection (LOD) was calculated to be \(0.8\,\mathrm{pg\,mL^{-1}}\) via the equation \(\mathrm{LOD}=3\times\sigma\,/\,\mathrm{S}\), and the RSD for the determination of \(10\,\mathrm{ng\,mL^{-1}}\) ZEN was \(3.6\%\) (\(\mathbf{n}=11\)). Compared to the reported literatures on the quantitative detection of ZEN listed in Table S3, the developed method offered a relatively wider linear range and a lower LOD, which might be attributable to its delicate design, the satisfactory catalytic performance and stability of bimetallic MOFzyme and the appealing stimuli-responsiveness of DNA hydrogel. Apart from that, compared to the surface plasmon resonance (SPR) biosensor, which required expensive instrumentations, or electrochemical and immunological methods, which required cumbersome procedures, the developed method required no centrifugation and only filtration before collecting the colorimetric signals that can be directly observed by the naked eye, whilst effectively
398 guaranteeing its specificity.
