## 3.9 Selectivity of the developed aptasensor
3.9 Selectivity of the developed aptasensorThe selectivity of the biosensing platform was evaluated by exploiting the effect of other mycotoxins on the quantification of ZEN spiked in PBS, corn and soybean samples. As depicted in Fig. 5C, in the absence of ZEN, other mycotoxins whose concentrations were ten times that of ZEN caused little discernible change in the absorbance of the system in PBS. Similar results were observed in corn and soybean samples (Fig. S3A and B). Moreover, samples spiked simultaneously with \(10\;\mathrm{ng\;mL^{-1}}\) of ZEN and \(100\;\mathrm{ng\;mL^{-1}}\) of other mycotoxins, whether in buffer solution or food matrices, produced little change in absorbance compared to ZEN-only samples (Fig. 5D, S3C and D). Overall, the developed method demonstrated highly qualified selectivity towards other mycotoxins that frequently coexist with ZEN.
## 3.10 Validation and application
3.10 Validation and applicationNaturally contaminated certified reference material (CRM) for ZEN was adopted for method validation. The ZEN content of the CRM was determined to be \(62.080\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\), which was in good conformity with the certified value \((65.0 \pm 13.2\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1})\). Moreover, the RSD of repeatability (RSD\(_r\)) \((n = 18)\) and of within-laboratory reproducibility (RSD\(_{\text{WLR}}\)) \((n = 10)\) were calculated to be \(6.1\%\) and \(7.4\%\), respectively. The validation parameters satisfied the criteria laid down by the European Union (EU).
Subsequently, the applicability of the developed method was authenticated in food
matrices, including corn and soybean samples (Table S4). Except for soybean 1, other samples were detected with ZEN to varying degrees. The recoveries for corn samples fell within the range of \(94.0\%-109.0\%\). While for soybean samples, the recoveries ranged from \(95.2\%\) to \(108.0\%\). In addition, the highest RSD \((n=6)\) value obtained among all samples was \(6.4\%\).
Previously, an aptasensor developed based on DNA hybrid duplex-functionalized MOF nano-container (UiO-66-NH\(_2\) (Zr)) and trivalent G4-DNAzyme for the specific detection of ZEN was reported by our group (Sun et al., 2022). Unlike the strategy developed here (using bimetallic MOFzyme), the signal generation and amplification of which depended on the successful assembly of the trivalent DNA peroxidase mimicking enzyme (DNAzyme) and therefore required more time for detection (over 2 h). As for the aptasensor developed in this study, since the target recognition elements (aptamers in the DNA hydrogel) and the signal generation cum amplification components (MOFzyme) were integrated together, no additional materials other than the sample and organic substrate were necessary. The target-induced disintegration of the hydrogel and the color development process can be performed simultaneously, eliminating the need for lengthy incubation procedures. Besides, only a filtration operation was required after the reaction was completed before measuring the response signals, which further shortened the assay times. The quantitative results can be obtained within 35 min, for a total time of less than 45
min when sample preparation time was taken into account. The above results further substantiated the reliability and ease of use of the developed method.
## 4. Conclusion
Overall, a novel and sensitive colorimetric aptasensor was developed based on the HA-DNA hydrogel-coated bimetallic MOFzyme to tackle the shortcomings of existing detection strategies. The doping of the bimetals brought satisfactory catalytic ability to the Ce-Zr bimetallic MOFzyme. Meanwhile, the two M(IV) nodes also effectively ensured the stability of the catalytic performance of the MOFzyme. In addition, the DNA hydrogel ideally retained the recognition sensitivity and specificity of the aptamer in it, and was therefore endowed with exceptional stimuli-responsiveness. Benefiting from its delicate design and the above-mentioned merits, the developed aptasensor displayed remarkable analytical performance and favorable ease of use. Notably, by substituting aptamer sequences, the developed method can be easily extrapolated to other analytes and promises to be a prospective platform for rapidly and accurately quantifying food or environmental hazards.
